    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Toyota Crown Signia Gets GT-S Concept Treatment for SEMA, Becomes a Sleek Wagon\nGM Pulls the Plug on the Corvette SUV Project\n8 photos\nPhoto: Evren Ozgun Spy Sketch | YouTube\nGeneral Motors has reportedly pulled the plug on the SUV Corvette that the auto giant was planning to launch as part of a lineup expansion. The project has been shelved until further notice. Translation: until the market becomes more stable.\nGeneral Motors has reportedly scrapped plans to expand the Corvette lineup into a standalone sub-brand, putting an end to the development of a long-rumored all-electric crossover SUV , a four-door sedan, and a pure EV sports car.\nThe decision marks a major strategic shift for the auto giant, which has been quietly working behind the scenes on the ambitious lineup expansion slated to launch around the 2025 - 2026 calendar years and postponed until it was completely canceled.\nCodenamed V224, with some sources noting V284, but none officially confirmed, the all-electric crossover was envisioned as a high-volume, high-performance model, aimed directly at the Porsche Cayenne.\nCiting sources familiar with the matter, GM Authority reports that the core model variants should have been underpinned by a newly developed premium BEV Prime architecture. The same platform would have been used for an electric two-door sports car .\nPhoto: Evren Ozgun Spy Sketch | YouTube In the meantime, a four-door electric sedan with a fastback-like silhouette, bearing the Corvette badge and different power versions, was also part of the plan for an end-of-the-decade launch, targeting competition with the Porsche Taycan.\nChevrolet hasn't confirmed the cancellation, as it hasn't disclosed any details when the models were supposedly in development, so there is no official explanation for the move. However, we can easily read between the lines and blame the plunging EV demand, especially in the performance segment, over the past year.\nFurthermore, expanding the lineup with models that were unlike anything before it would have been massive cause for backlash. Just remember what Ferrari had to deal with when unveiling its first-ever EV , the Luce , earlier this year, or what Porsche went through when it rolled out its first SUV, the Cayenne, back in 2022.\nFor the moment, the Corvette lineup remains as you know it, starting with the base Stingray with the 6.2-liter LT2 V8 producing 490 horsepower (497 metric horsepower) and going all the way to the hybrid ZR1X , with its mind-blowing 1,250 horsepower (1,268 metric horsepower).\nPhoto: Evren Ozgun Spy Sketch | YouTube No EV, no SUV, and no sedan will intrude in the lineup for the moment, as General Motors would rather focus on developing the C9 generation instead of coming up with new models for the C8. However, the C9 won't drop before 2029 as a 2030 model year.\nIn the meantime, electrification might be contagious for other models of the Corvette portfolio. For the moment, only the Grand Sport X and the ZR1X feature an electric motor and all-wheel drive. GM Authority notes that an electric two-door Corvette project might be very much alive, even though there is no active vehicle program associated with it.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChevrolet Corvette Chevrolet Corvette SUV chevrolet corvette sedan Chevrolet Corvette EV Chevrolet General Motors\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT Dealer Buys a 1964 Plymouth Barracuda V8 Landau for $23,000, Flips It for $64,000 35 Days Later",
